<div class="key-takeaways"><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul>
<li>Port Arthur has been hit by wind (Rita, Humberto, Laura, Delta), storm surge (Ike), extreme rain (Harvey, Imelda), and a hard freeze (February 2021) within about 15 years.</li>
<li>Each type of storm creates a different kind of insurance claim: TWIA wind, NFIP or private flood, or homeowners water damage.</li>
<li>The levee and seawall system reduces surge risk but does not stop rainfall flooding, as Harvey and Imelda showed.</li>
<li>Many local claim problems come from coverage gaps (no flood policy), percentage deductibles, and repairs that did not meet TDI windstorm requirements.</li>
<li>NOAA and NWS records help document date and cause of loss for any storm claim.</li>
</ul></div>

<p>Port Arthur hurricane history reads like a catalog of every way a Gulf storm can damage a building. Some storms came with wind that peeled roofs and dropped trees on houses. One pushed the highest surge ever recorded at Sabine Pass. Two others barely registered as wind events but flooded tens of thousands of homes with rain. Then came a freeze that burst pipes in a city built for heat.</p>

<p>For homeowners and business owners in Port Arthur, Groves, Nederland, Port Neches, Sabine Pass and the rest of Jefferson County, those storms are more than history. They show what your insurance needs to handle and where claims tend to break down. This article walks through the major events since 2005, using National Hurricane Center, National Weather Service and NOAA Storm Events records, and pulls out the insurance lesson from each. For a full list of local records, see our <a href="/storm-history/">storm history</a> page.</p>

<h2>Port Arthur hurricane history at a glance</h2>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Storm</th><th>Date</th><th>Main local impact</th><th>Insurance lesson</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
