<tr><td>Winter Storm</td><td>February 2021</td><td>Prolonged freeze; burst pipes</td><td>Not every disaster is a hurricane; water damage rules apply</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>Hurricane Rita (2005): the wind storm that defined a generation of claims</h2>

<p>Hurricane Rita made landfall on September 24, 2005 near the Texas/Louisiana border as a Category 3, according to the National Hurricane Center. NOAA's Storm Events Database describes Rita moving northwest across Southeast Texas that morning with sustained winds of 120 mph. Storm surge near 10 feet hit the Sabine Pass area, where over 90 percent of homes were severely damaged or destroyed. Winds over 100 mph blew across the entire region, snapping and uprooting trees.</p>

<p><strong>Insurance lesson:</strong> Rita was primarily a wind event for most of Port Arthur. That put roofs, siding, windows, and trees at the center of claims, and it made the wind policy, often TWIA, the key coverage. Owners who had clear photos of the roof before the storm, and who documented every opening after, were in a stronger position when insurers questioned whether damage was old wear and tear. It also showed how quickly contractors and materials become scarce after a regional catastrophe, which affects pricing in estimates. If you are working a <a href="/services/wind-damage-claims/">wind damage claim</a> today, the same principle applies: document the opening and tie it to the date.</p>

<h2>Hurricane Humberto (2007): small storm, real damage</h2>

<p>Humberto developed quickly and came ashore around 1 a.m. on September 13, 2007 near High Island. NOAA records place landfall in rural southwestern Jefferson County, in the McFaddin National Wildlife Refuge, with maximum winds estimated at 90 mph. Hurricane-force winds were confined to a band about 15 miles wide, but Port Arthur, Sabine Pass and Nederland saw winds of 60 to 80 mph. Damage was primarily downed trees, roof damage, and downed power lines.</p>

<p><strong>Insurance lesson:</strong> A compact storm can still cause a roof claim. With small storms, insurers sometimes question whether high winds actually reached a specific street. Weather data and storm reports for the date of loss help. Humberto is also a reminder that a percentage hurricane deductible can swallow a moderate roof claim, so knowing your deductible in dollars matters. Our guide to <a href="/hurricane-deductible-texas-explained/">Texas hurricane deductibles</a> explains the math.</p>

<h2>Hurricane Ike (2008): the surge storm</h2>

<p>Ike made landfall at Galveston on September 13, 2008 as a Category 2, well southwest of Port Arthur. Its size pushed water far up the coast. According to NOAA's Storm Events Database, a 14 to 15 foot storm surge at Sabine Pass produced the highest water level ever recorded there; any home that was not elevated was destroyed. The surge did not top the Port Arthur seawall, but wave action on top of the surge did, and at least 100 homes flooded in Port Arthur. Water backed up Hillebrandt and Taylor Bayous, flooding Hillebrandt, Hamshire and Fannett, and pushed up the Neches River in Beaumont. At least 4,000 homes flooded countywide. A separate NOAA entry describes Category 1 wind damage across Jefferson County.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/photos/storm-damaged-neighborhood-roofs.webp" alt="Neighborhood of homes with storm-damaged roofs after a hurricane" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Large storms like Ike can leave the same neighborhood with both wind damage to roofs and surge or flood damage below.</figcaption></figure>

<p><strong>Insurance lesson:</strong> Ike is the textbook mixed-cause storm. A single property could have a tree through the roof from wind and several feet of surge inside. Surge is flood, so it is excluded from homeowners and TWIA policies and covered only by flood insurance. Each insurer sends its own adjuster and applies its own deductible, and each may try to attribute disputed damage to the other cause. Our article on <a href="/wind-vs-flood-damage-port-arthur-insurance/">wind vs. flood damage in Port Arthur</a> covers how to document that split.</p>

<h2>Hurricane Harvey (2017): the rain storm</h2>

<p>For Port Arthur, Harvey was not about wind. NOAA records show tropical storm conditions along the Jefferson County coast and a modest surge at Texas Point. The disaster was rain. According to the National Weather Service in Lake Charles, heavy rain fell over Jefferson County from August 24 to 31, 2017, with widespread storm totals over 40 inches. The highest storm total from Harvey was 60.58 inches near Nederland, a U.S. tropical cyclone rainfall record at the time. More than 64,000 homes flooded in Jefferson County, with Port Arthur, Groves and northeast Beaumont among the hardest hit, and Pine Island Bayou and the lower Neches River reached record crests.</p>

<p><strong>Insurance lesson:</strong> Rising water from rain is flood. Standard homeowners and TWIA policies generally exclude it, so owners without NFIP or private flood coverage often had no policy that responded to the main damage. Harvey also showed that the levee system does not protect against rainfall flooding inside the city. For those who did have flood insurance, the details mattered: separate building and contents limits, strict proof of loss requirements, and careful documentation of contents. If your property is anywhere in Jefferson County, flood coverage is worth discussing with your agent, keeping in mind that NFIP policies generally have a 30-day waiting period.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>Tropical Storm Imelda (2019): it can happen again</h2>

<p>Two years after Harvey, Tropical Storm Imelda stalled over Southeast Texas from September 17 to 19, 2019. Preliminary totals exceeded 40 inches in parts of Jefferson County, and flash flooding spread across the region, including the Port Arthur and Beaumont areas. The NWS Houston office ranks Imelda among the wettest tropical cyclones in Texas.</p>

<p><strong>Insurance lesson:</strong> Owners who flooded in Harvey and did not buy flood coverage afterward were exposed again. Imelda also flooded properties that did not flood in Harvey, a reminder that flood maps reflect probabilities, not guarantees. A "weak" tropical storm with little wind can still produce a total loss from water. Check whether you have flood coverage before the season, not when a storm is named.</p>

<h2>Hurricanes Laura and Delta (2020): back-to-back storms</h2>

<p>Laura made landfall on August 27, 2020 near Cameron, Louisiana, as a Category 4, roughly 30 miles east of the Texas border. NOAA records for Jefferson County describe a mandatory evacuation, storm surge flooding at Sabine Pass, and water backing up the Neches River, Hillebrandt Bayou and Taylor Bayou. Numerous trees and power lines came down across Port Arthur, Port Neches, Beaumont, Nederland and Groves, damaging homes and businesses, and over 60 percent of the county lost power at the peak. Tragically, NOAA also records indirect deaths from carbon monoxide poisoning, including three in Port Arthur from a generator running in an enclosed area.</p>

<p>Six weeks later, Hurricane Delta came ashore on October 9, 2020 near Creole, Louisiana. NOAA records sustained winds of 70 knots with a gust to 87 knots at Sabine Pass, and a gust to 78 knots at Jack Brooks Regional Airport, with 5 to 6.5 inches of rain across the county.</p>

<p><strong>Insurance lesson:</strong> When two storms hit the same area weeks apart, insurers may ask which storm caused which damage, and whether damage from the first storm was mitigated before the second. Separate dates of loss can mean separate claims and separate deductibles. Owners who photographed and tarped after Laura, and kept receipts, had a cleaner record when Delta arrived. And never run a generator inside a house or garage.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/photos/public-adjuster-roof-inspection-tarp.webp" alt="Public adjuster inspecting a tarped roof after a hurricane" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Prompt tarping and photos after one storm help separate its damage from the next.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>The February 2021 freeze: not every disaster is a hurricane</h2>

<p>In February 2021, a prolonged freeze gripped Texas and pipes burst statewide, including across the Golden Triangle. Many Southeast Texas homes have plumbing in exterior walls, attics, or crawlspaces under pier-and-beam floors, which leaves pipes exposed in a hard freeze.</p>

<p><strong>Insurance lesson:</strong> Freeze losses are usually handled as sudden water damage under the homeowners or commercial policy, not TWIA or flood. Policies may include conditions about maintaining heat, and humidity here means mold can follow quickly if drying is delayed. See our <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a> page for how those claims are typically evaluated.</p>

<h2>Recent reminders: Beryl and beyond</h2>

<p>The pattern continues. When Hurricane Beryl moved through on July 8, 2024, NOAA recorded a maximum sustained wind of 55 mph and a peak gust of 70 mph at Sabine Pass, with storm surge covering much of Highway 87 between Sabine Pass and Sea Rim State Park. Severe thunderstorms and tornadoes add to the list; NOAA records an EF-2 tornado in Port Arthur on April 10, 2024. Track current systems on our <a href="/hurricane-tracker/">hurricane tracker</a> and browse recent local events on the <a href="/weather-events/">weather events</a> page.</p>

