<h2>Five lessons that apply to every Port Arthur storm</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>Know which policy covers which peril.</strong> In Jefferson County, wind is often TWIA, flood is NFIP or private, and everything else is homeowners. One storm can mean two or three claims.</li>
<li><strong>Know your deductibles in dollars.</strong> Percentage wind and hurricane deductibles can be thousands of dollars on a typical home.</li>
<li><strong>Repair to TDI windstorm standards.</strong> Re-roofs and roof repairs in the catastrophe area must be inspected to TDI specifications to stay eligible for TWIA coverage, and city permits generally apply. See our <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a> page.</li>
<li><strong>Document before and after.</strong> Pre-storm photos of the roof and interior, and detailed post-storm photos, often decide disputes about old vs. new damage.</li>
<li><strong>Use the weather record.</strong> NOAA and NWS data can confirm wind, surge, and rainfall on your date of loss.</li>
</ol>

<h2>How a Port Arthur Public Adjuster uses storm history</h2>

<p>Storm records are part of claim documentation. When an insurer questions whether wind reached a property, or whether damage came from this storm or an older one, NOAA and NWS data for the date and location can help answer it. A Port Arthur Public Adjuster can pull those records, inspect and document the property, prepare a detailed estimate, and present the claim to each insurer involved. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. No outcome is guaranteed, and coverage depends on your policy and the facts.</p>

<p>If a storm has damaged your property, start with our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a>, or ask for a Port Arthur Public Adjuster review of your <a href="/services/hurricane-damage-claims/">hurricane damage claim</a>.</p>
